LOGBOOK.md:812:- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with a non-empty list of canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. The earlier one-to-four endpoint ceiling and 5/5/15/10-second, 32/128-query values had no accepted evidence and are removed. New TASK-260721-3miqh4 owns measured endpoint/message/byte/capacity/timing defaults and hard caps; schema, transport, relay, and M4 consumers are blocked on its independently accepted `DNSRuntimePolicyV1`.
LOGBOOK.md:815:- SAFETY/PLANNING: Resolver/policy validation and SSH `direct-tcpip` readiness precede route/DNS settings. Post-settings ordinary queries have only the virtual ingress -> authenticated SSH/relay -> explicit endpoint path. Endpoint exhaustion returns bounded SERVFAIL, withdraws safe-DNS capability, and clears settings; it cannot call the device resolver. Missing legacy resolver data yields `requiresResolverConfiguration` with no inferred migration. TASK-260721-33o8fc owns schema/codec/migration, TASK-260721-2raag7 owns M4 profile experience, and revised TASK-260715-5o6jqg/TASK-260715-28jdml/TASK-260715-336ljl own shared-failure, M2, and integration evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:819:- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
LOGBOOK.md:822:- VERIFICATION: Eighteen admission tests cover below/at/above every independent ceiling, 128/256-way reserve races, 500 release/open and 500 late-callback races, 250 explicit-release/deinit ownership races, ID exhaustion, queued-byte overflow, session-health races, 8,000 sustained pressure rejects alongside 2,000 admitted-flow progress steps, every terminal reason across 320 churn lifecycles, and exact local/exported baseline recovery. The bounded-queue convergence probe passes 20/20 runs; focused normal and TSan admission/diagnostics suites pass; `make validate-core` passes boundary/native gates, 306 tests in 27 suites, and the post-test build; strict recursive Swift format, privacy/boundary, diff, and board validation pass.
LOGBOOK.md:832:- BOUNDARY: Wrong/stale capability and the M1 monotonic accept-to-auth deadline remain authentication-phase failures with only phase-and-remaining-budget-safe replies. The post-handoff adapter resource reservation is separate from the boundary pending-authentication ceiling.
LOGBOOK.md:837:- CORRECTION: Current `HEVSOCKSBoundary` uses `SO_RCVTIMEO`, which is a per-receive inactivity bound and can restart as bytes arrive; authentication sends have no deadline. The pending set is count-bounded, but current source does not prove an absolute accept-to-auth deadline. M1 must compute one monotonic deadline at accept, apply its remaining budget across greeting/auth reads, comparisons, and replies without progress extension, and prove slow-trickle/reply-stall eviction plus slot/descriptor release on iOS and macOS.
LOGBOOK.md:851:- REWORK: Normalize thrown NetworkExtension start errors at the thin adapter boundary (or through an equivalently shared injectable translator), preserve unknown domain/code, cover both iOS/macOS seams plus the disabled-manager production path, and rerun focused normal/TSan, full core, formatting/diff/board checks, and both adapter builds before another reviewer cycle.
LOGBOOK.md:861:- REGRESSION: Both NetworkExtension adapters read `NEVPNConnection.status` before installing `NEVPNStatusDidChange`, so a terminal transition in that gap could be lost and an already-stopped session could falsely reach the 15-second `stopTimedOut` path.
LOGBOOK.md:867:- REGRESSION: Actor isolation did not serialize repository calls across suspended NetworkExtension callbacks, so concurrent operations could load and mutate overlapping manager generations. Both platform seams also used `NSNumber.intValue`, truncating `1.5` to current version 1 and overflowing `UInt64.max` into repairable corruption.
LOGBOOK.md:879:- DECISION: `ReluxTunnelCore` owns the serialized exact-predicate repository, immutable canonical configuration, 15-second callback tokens, stable errors, one-stale retry, and reload verification. The iOS/macOS adapter modules only translate public `NetworkExtension` objects and notifications into the injectable core seams.
LOGBOOK.md:888:- SCHEMA: Provider stop metadata extends diagnostics v1 to 97 counters, 57 gauges, and 11 finite error codes across the same 10 domains. The schema still retains no endpoints, DNS names, traffic identifiers, secrets, configuration, localized errors, or free-form labels.
LOGBOOK.md:889:- COVERAGE: Shared tests run through iOS and macOS seams for all four commands, hostile/oversized/future/mutating inputs, duplicates, nil handlers, retirement, all raw Apple reasons, startup and cleanup deadlines, stop-wins-start, fatal handoff, deallocation, and two 100-cycle baselines. Existing coordinator tests retain every startup acquisition/cancellation point and route-clear uncertainty coverage.
LOGBOOK.md:898:- DECISION: Fault injection uses the coordinator's injected cancellation-check seam to fail at fourteen exact before/after ownership boundaries. This keeps production code unchanged while proving reverse rollback, one disconnecting publication, one terminal publication, exact error mapping, and single cleanup for configuration, SSH, TCP, DNS, packet plane, settings, and packet-read ownership.
LOGBOOK.md:899:- COVERAGE: The explicit control table covers rejected duplicate starts, idempotent stops, healthy/stale/late callbacks in usable, stopping, disconnected, and failed states; phase gates now include configuration as well as SSH, TCP, DNS, packet preparation, settings apply, packet activation, and final health. All four mandatory dependency failures must revoke capability before cleanup begins.
LOGBOOK.md:913:- COVERAGE: A package-internal async handoff hook, following the existing core test-hook pattern, deterministically injects current-generation DNS health loss after child completion and independently pauses the same boundary for caller cancellation. Neither test issues a masking `stop()`; both assert no capability revival, exactly-once reverse cleanup, a zero external resource ledger, and the coordinator baseline footprint.
LOGBOOK.md:918:- SAFETY: Settings are requested only after validated configuration, authenticated SSH, TCP readiness, SSH-only safe DNS readiness, pure packet-plane preparation, and mandatory health checks. Unknown apply failures are conservatively treated as an uncertain commit and cleared; definite non-commit skips clear. A clear failure publishes TCP/DNS false while truthfully retaining `routesInstalled=true` and `routeState=clearFailed`.
LOGBOOK.md:919:- CLEANUP: Teardown closes admission, stops packet reads/native work, clears settings when required, then stops DNS, TCP, and SSH in reverse acquisition order. The coordinator releases configuration references and all tracked task/resource handles; repeated stop calls join the same cleanup.
LOGBOOK.md:930:- DECISION: `RuntimeDiagnosticsStore` owns one bounded generation with 96 counters, 56 gauges, one cumulative DNS-latency histogram, and at most one cataloged error per each of 10 finite domains. Packet/HEV use its `TunnelMetrics` recorder, SSH uses the same recorder without lane identity, and coordinator/TCP/DNS/route/memory use typed finite-category updates; unknown free-form metric names are counted then discarded without retaining the label.
LOGBOOK.md:932:- PRIVACY: The default schema has no lane/run/flow IDs, DNS or destination fields, addresses, credentials, commands/stdin, payload content, or traffic samples. State transition times are monotonic process-uptime milliseconds; errors retain only validated domain/code; flow, memory, route, health, packet/byte, drop-reason, DNS-result, and SSH values are aggregates.
LOGBOOK.md:939:- SAFETY: A future lifecycle `routeState` previously decoded as `unknown` while preserving true TCP, safe-DNS, route-installed, and health facts. Route state now participates in the shared fail-safe projection for both decoded and directly constructed lifecycle snapshots, forcing every capability false when it is unknown.
LOGBOOK.md:946:- CAPABILITY: TCP, safe DNS, UDP, route mode, route-installed state, and health are independent required facts with no full-mode bit. Unknown output lifecycle/route values project to `unknown` with every capability false; generation/sequence ordering rejects lower generations and non-increasing snapshots.
LOGBOOK.md:950:- DECISION: The packet-tunnel extension owns one actor-serialized runtime generation, with immutable app-message snapshots and no live forwarding dependency on the containing app. Startup is configuration -> verified/authenticated SSH -> TCP/DNS readiness -> pure packet preflight -> settings apply -> existing PacketBridge.start/read activation -> TCP + safe-DNS publication; every failure rolls back through one idempotent, non-cancellable stopping path.
LOGBOOK.md:951:- OWNERSHIP: M1PacketPlaneSession is the sole owner of the HEV private SOCKS boundary and dispatches authenticated CONNECT to TCP plus only approved virtual-DNS UDP/TCP to DNS; other UDP is rejected until M2. The coordinator proceeds against fakes independently of production factories, and both branches converge at TASK-260715-30ugfm.
LOGBOOK.md:953:- SAFETY: Settings clear is called only after a committed or uncertain apply. A required failed/uncertain clear publishes capabilities false but truthfully retains routesInstalled=true with routeState=clearFailed; a clear that was unnecessary or confirmed publishes routesInstalled=false. Legacy version protocol v1 remains byte-exact while new M1 commands/snapshots use explicit schemas.
LOGBOOK.md:954:- REVIEW: Independent agent review requested and verified corrections across ownership, source-interface feasibility, versions, dependencies, transitions, and route cleanup, then accepted all five AC. PlantUML is unavailable and local Graphviz is broken by missing libltdl; authoritative sources and the toolchain anomaly are attached rather than claiming rendered evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:977:- DECISION: The codec preserves HEV domain bytes as an opaque 1–248-byte wire value, including non-ASCII and NUL. DNS presentation-form, invalid A-label, raw-IP-text, and IDNA policy remains exclusively in the later Go resolution gate; neither codec normalizes, resolves, or logs domain bytes. The endpoint type is direction-neutral so a response encodes the relay-observed source rather than substituting the original destination.
LOGBOOK.md:982:- IMPLEMENTATION: Swift and Go now encode the four-byte big-endian body length plus type/flags/association ID as separate header/payload write slices, and decode arbitrary partial/coalesced stream reads while retaining at most one negotiated frame plus the fixed prefix. Prefix bounds are checked before the first body allocation; generated message metadata drives direction, association-ID, fixed-payload, reserved-flag, and negotiated DNS-priority validation.
